= Energy =

== Overview ==
'''Energy''' is a quantitative physical property of a system associated with its state and capacity to produce changes. In mechanics it can be transferred by work; more generally it can be transferred by heating, radiation, matter flow or other interactions. The SI unit of energy is the joule (J).

== Kinetic and potential energy ==
'''Kinetic energy''' is associated with motion relative to a chosen reference frame. For a classical particle of constant mass m moving with speed v, its kinetic energy is K = (1/2)mv². '''Potential energy''' can describe the position or configuration of interacting parts of a system; for example, gravitational and elastic potential energy.

Other useful physical descriptions include chemical, electrical, thermal and electromagnetic energy. These labels are contextual descriptions of energy stored or carried by different physical mechanisms, not separate substances.

== Conservation ==
In an appropriately defined isolated physical system, total energy is conserved. Energy can be transferred and transformed even when the energy associated with a useful service decreases. For instance, braking converts much of a vehicle's kinetic energy into thermal energy; the energy has not simply vanished.

The boundary of the system and the accounting of energy carried across it are essential to an application of the conservation principle. Mechanical energy alone can decrease because of friction even while total physical energy is conserved in a wider accounting.

== Energy versus power and momentum ==
Power is the rate of energy transfer, expressed in watts (joules per second). The same energy can be transferred quickly or slowly, so power and energy are not synonymous. [[Related to::Momentum|Momentum]] is a vector quantity that follows a different conservation law; zero net momentum does not imply zero kinetic energy.

== Conversion and practical use ==
[[Related to::Energy conversion|Energy conversion]] is the transformation of energy between physical forms or carriers. [[Related to::Energy efficiency|Energy efficiency]] assesses how much useful output a system delivers per input under a stated boundary. Neither engineering efficiency nor energy conservation in the policy sense should be confused with the physical conservation law.

== Evidence and limits ==
Numerical comparisons require specified systems, reference frames, measurement conditions and units. Thermodynamic, electromagnetic, relativistic or quantum descriptions may be needed outside simple classical-mechanics examples.

== Thermal processes and the first law ==
[[Related to::Thermodynamics|Thermodynamics]] describes energy transfers by heating and work under declared system conventions. For a closed system, the internal-energy change can be expressed as heat added minus work done by the system. Heat is a process of energy transfer caused by a temperature difference, not an independent substance permanently stored inside a body. Temperature, internal energy and heat transfer are distinct quantities.


